# Line endings are pinned here rather than left to core.autocrlf.
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` alone was not enough, and the failure was invisible: git
# classifies a file as binary if it contains a NUL byte or a single lone CR
# (0x0D not followed by 0x0A) anywhere in it, and a binary file skips the clean
# filter entirely. One lone CR was enough to make `git add` store CRLF verbatim,
# with a plausible whole-file diff and no "CRLF will be replaced by LF" warning.
# That happened to docs/UPSTREAM_ISSUES.md (8 lone CRs from doubled-CR paragraph
# breaks) and to a raw NUL in src/main/ipc/project.recent.test.ts.
#
# `eol=lf` is load-bearing rather than cosmetic. Measured on a throwaway clone:
# `text=auto` ALONE still routes through the binary check, and forced 156 files
# to CRLF including every pure-LF agent SKILL.md file — reproducing exactly the
# incident AGENTS.md records. Pinning the EOL on the worktree side is what
# actually fixes it.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The NUL is gone from this file on main, but 16 of 24 remote branches still
# carry it in their committed blob, and a checkout will bring it back to those.
# `-text` declares the status quo rather than repairing an existing blob.
src/main/ipc/project.recent.test.ts -text
